---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/foundational/ecosystems-and-biomes">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An ecosystem is a working machine: organisms plus the physical conditions they live in,
    linked by two flows — <strong>energy</strong> running one way from sunlight through plants
    to consumers, and <strong>matter</strong> cycling round and round. At the largest scale,
    similar climates produce similar ecosystems: biomes. The syllabus (topics 3.1–3.4) demands
    two in depth, and they are the two most extreme answers to the same question — how to run
    life on Earth's energy budget. The <strong>Antarctic ecosystem</strong> (syllabus 3.1.1)
    is the continent of Antarctica and the Southern Ocean around it: about 14 million km² of
    rock and ice carrying over 2 km of ice on average, holding roughly 90% of all the ice on
    Earth. The <strong>tropical rainforest</strong> (3.3.1) bands the equator in the Amazon,
    the Congo Basin and South-East Asia, and packs more species into a hectare than Antarctica
    has in its entire two flowering plants.
  </p>

  <h2>Two climates, one engine</h2>
  <p>
    Both climates are set by the same geometry. In Antarctica (3.1.2), high latitude means the
    sun barely lifts above the horizon even at midsummer; its energy spreads thin, cold dense
    air sinks and pools as <strong>high pressure</strong>, and precipitation is so sparse that
    the interior is technically a polar desert — under 50 mm of water equivalent a year. Vostok
    station's annual mean is about −55 °C; the world's lowest recorded surface temperature,
    −89.2 °C, was measured there on 21 July 1983. In the rainforest (3.3.2) the near-overhead
    sun beats down all year, heating moist air which rises in <strong>low pressure</strong>
    convection and dumps afternoon thunderstorms: Manaus at 3°S averages around 27 °C every
    month and over 2,200 mm of rain a year.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="noon sun elevation through a year: the Amazon's steady high sun vs the Antarctic's skimming light (below zero = polar night)" widget="plotter" config={sunCfg} />

  <p>
    Pressure belts also write the winds (3.1.4, 3.3.4). Air spiralling off the polar high
    accelerates downhill to the coast as <strong>katabatic winds</strong> — Cape Denison,
    where Douglas Mawson wintered in 1912, is the windiest place at sea level on Earth, with an
    annual mean around 70–80 km/h. At the equator, air rushing into the low-pressure belt is
    deflected west as the <strong>trade winds</strong>. Drag the arrow below: map view, right
    is east, up is north — length is speed, and the readouts give the components.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="map-view wind vector: drag it — a southerly howler for an Antarctic coastal station, then a gentle easterly trade for the rainforest" widget="diagramBuilder" config={windCfg} />

  <h2>Life at the extremes</h2>
  <p>
    Antarctica's land is nearly sterile, but its ocean teems (3.1.3). Diatoms feed
    <strong>krill</strong>, shrimp-like animals a few centimetres long that swarm in shoals
    visible from space; krill feed squid, fish, penguins, seals and whales — the keystone of
    the whole web. Adaptations are fierce: krill shrink and starve through winter under the
    ice; emperor penguins incubate their egg through midwinter darkness, males huddling at
    −40 °C and fasting for up to four months; icefish carry natural antifreeze glycoproteins
    in their blood. The rainforest answers differently (3.3.3, 3.3.4): structure stacked in
    layers — emergents past 50 m, a light-hungry canopy, a dim understorey, a floor where
    buttress roots prop giants in thin, poor soil. The nutrients do not live in the ground;
    they live in the biomass, recycled so fast by fungi and falling litter that clearing the
    forest leaves ground good for pasture for only a few years.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="the 10% rule: why a food web that feeds the largest animals on Earth must stand on hundreds of millions of tonnes of krill" widget="sliderLab" config={trophicCfg} />

  <p>
    Only about 10% of the energy at each level survives the trip up (roughly the syllabus's
    interrelationships idea, 3.1.4/3.3.4, made arithmetic). That is why blue whales — the
    largest animals ever — eat several tonnes of krill a day, and why 3.2.2's warning bites:
    if krill collapse, everything above it feels the shortage.
  </p>

  <h2>The Antarctic food web, properly built</h2>
  <p>
    The web has a calendar, and the calendar is the sea ice. Each austral spring, as ice
    melts and sunlight returns, the Southern Ocean blooms: diatoms — single-celled algae
    that live <em>inside</em> and under the ice — photosynthesize in such dense carpets that
    the ice underside turns brown. Krill (Euphausia superba), the shrimp-like grazers, spawn
    in deep water but their larvae need the ice's underside as both nursery and cafeteria,
    grazing the diatom film through winter. Krill swarm in shoals that can stretch for
    kilometres, and the Southern Ocean's total krill biomass — several hundred million
    tonnes, comparable to the entire human population's mass — is probably the largest
    standing stock of any single wild species on Earth. Everything above it is scaled to
    that base: squid and silverfish, then penguin colonies (chinstraps and Adélies time
    their chick-rearing to krill availability), fur and crabeater seals, and the great
    whales, which feed all summer and fast on blubber for months. Two land facts complete
    the picture: Antarctica's terrestrial life is almost humble — lichens and mosses, two
    flowering plants (Antarctic hair grass and pearlwort), and microbes — because the land
    is a polar desert with no soil to speak of; and everything that "belongs" there is an
    <em>adaptation specialist</em>, which is exactly why arrivals from elsewhere (rats on
    South Georgia, grass colonising the warmed Peninsula) find so little competition.
  </p>
  <p>
    The adaptations are the exam's favourite evidence, and each has a mechanism. Emperor
    penguins breed <em>on</em> the winter ice: the male incubates the single egg on his feet
    for roughly four months of darkness, fasting entirely, in huddles that physics keeps at
    a balmy interior temperature — individuals rotate from the −40 °C rim to the warm core,
    a self-organising convection scheme. Weddell seals keep breathing holes open with their
    teeth. Antarctic icefish have cleared the protein haemoglobin from their blood entirely
    — the cold water holds enough dissolved oxygen — and instead stock glycoprotein
    "antifreeze" that binds to invading ice crystals and stops them growing. And krill
    themselves can <em>shrink</em> on demand, down-moulting through foodless winters — a
    life-history trick almost unique to them, matched to an ecosystem that shuts down for
    half a year.
  </p>

  <h2>The rainforest's answer: diversity as strategy</h2>
  <p>
    Where Antarctica survives by specialising, the rainforest diversifies — 10,000-plus
    species per square kilometre is not an exaggeration in Amazonian tree surveys, and a
    single hectare can hold more tree species than all of Europe. The mechanism is structure:
    every layer is a separate habitat with its own lighting, humidity and fauna, so the
    ecosystem stacks niches vertically instead of spreading them horizontally. The
    adaptations follow the layers. Drip-tip leaves shed water instantly (fungal rot is the
    constant enemy at 100% humidity); lianas — woody vines — commute to the canopy without
    paying for wood of their own, borrowing trees as scaffolding; epiphytes (orchids,
    bromeliads) live entirely on branch surfaces, their roots catching falling litter, and
    tank bromeliads hold whole ponds for frogs and mosquito larvae a hundred metres of
    climb above ground. Buttress roots exist because rainforest soil is shallow and nutrient-poor:
    with decomposition running in weeks, the nutrients live in the <em>living layer</em>,
    so the trees root wide, not deep, and propping them requires flying buttresses of wood.
    The web's most famous engineers are fig trees — fruiting year-round, they feed
    everything in lean seasons, and each fig species depends on one wasp species to
    pollinate it: a co-dependence so specific that losing one side collapses the other, a
    miniature of the whole ecosystem's fragility-in-diversity.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the two climates on paper</h2>
  <p>
    Put the two ecosystems on climate graphs and the contrast becomes data. Vostok's chart
    is a deep valley: every month below freezing, the warmest (January) near −32 °C and
    midwinter near −66 °C — a range of about 35 degrees with no month of melt, which is why no
    tree has ever grown there and the only "rain" falls as snow that never quite leaves.
    Manaus's chart is a plateau: every month between 26 and 28 °C (a range of 2 degrees —
    the equator's famous monotony) with 100–300 mm of rain each month and no dry season at
    all. Read any climate graph the same way — the <em>range</em> of the temperature line
    tells you the latitude story, the <em>total and timing</em> of the rain bars tells you
    the pressure-belt story — and these two ecosystems, the syllabus's chosen pair, are
    simply the two most extreme readings the planet offers.
  </p>

  <h2>Threats and impacts</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus lists the Antarctic's threats (3.2.1): <strong>resource exploitation</strong>
    (krill fishing now takes up to ~450,000 tonnes a year, against a precautionary 620,000
    tonne trigger limit), <strong>climate change</strong> (the Peninsula warmed by close to
    3 °C over the late 20th century; satellites reckon the ice sheet is losing around 150
    billion tonnes a year), <strong>fishing</strong> and surging <strong>tourism</strong>
    (just over 100,000 visitors in the 2022–23 season). The impacts of destruction (3.2.2)
    cascade: krill under-ice habitat lost means the pyramid in fig. 3 collapses upward; lost
    ice means darker ocean absorbing more heat; and the West Antarctic ice sheet alone holds
    roughly 3 m of sea-level rise.
  </p>
  <p>
    The named rainforest example (3.4.4) is the <strong>Brazilian Amazon</strong>. Threats
    (3.4.1): logging, road building (the Trans-Amazonian highway opened the interior),
    cattle ranching (most cleared land becomes pasture), soy farming, hydroelectric dams
    (Belo Monte displaced tens of thousands) and settlements. Impacts (3.4.2): about 17% of
    the original forest is gone; the Amazon recycles roughly half its own rainfall through
    'flying rivers', and scientists warn of a tipping point near 20–25% clearing, after which
    forest flips to savanna and its carbon returns to the sky.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing two commons: the mechanisms spelled out</h2>
  <p>
    Antarctic management (3.2.3) is a rare success of international law, and its pieces are
    worth naming precisely. The <strong>Antarctic Treaty</strong> (1959, in force 1961, now
    over 50 parties) freezes all territorial claims, reserves the continent for peace and
    science, and — crucially — bans military activity and nuclear testing. The
    <strong>Madrid Protocol</strong> (1991) bans mining outright (revisitable after 2048,
    which keeps the issue alive), requires environmental impact assessments for every
    activity, and mandates waste removal. <strong>CCAMLR</strong> (1982) runs
    ecosystem-based fisheries management: catches are set against the <em>whole food web</em>
    — the krill trigger limit of 620,000 tonnes exists explicitly to leave enough for
    penguins and seals — and includes the Ross Sea marine protected area (2016), roughly two
    million km², the largest on Earth. The machinery's weakness is its strength: every
    decision needs consensus, so proposals (like East Antarctic MPAs) can stall for years,
    and policing 14 million km² of ocean depends on flag states' goodwill. Tourism, oddly,
    is self-regulated: IAATO's industry rules (no closer than 5 m to wildlife, max 100
    ashore at once) are honoured precisely because operators profit from an intact
    Antarctica.
  </p>
  <p>
    Amazon management swings with politics, and the instruments are equally specific.
    <strong>Protected areas and indigenous territories</strong> are the workhorse — lands
    formally set aside (or titled to their Indigenous owners) clear at a fraction of the
    rate of unprotected land nearby, which makes Indigenous land titling the single most
    cost-effective conservation instrument in the basin. The <strong>2006 soy
    moratorium</strong> shows how markets can enforce rules traders once broke: grain
    merchants agreed not to buy soy grown on newly cleared forest, satellite monitoring
    made compliance checkable, and deforestation-for-soy collapsed. Norway's payments under
    <strong>REDD+</strong> (Reducing Emissions from Deforestation — paying Brazil per tonne
    of forest carbon not emitted) tested whether standing forest could out-earn cleared
    land. More than 140 countries pledged at Glasgow (2021) to halt and reverse
    deforestation by 2030 — a promise whose test will be satellite pixels, not signatures.
    The numbers prove policy works — annual clearing fell from ~27,700 km² (2004) to
    ~4,600 (2012) — and that it must be sustained: enforcement weakened after 2019 and
    clearing rebounded to ~11,600 km² by 2022 before falling again under renewed
    enforcement in 2023 (INPE PRODES, rounded). The two commons, read together, give the
    syllabus's management question its real answer: law, markets and local ownership all
    work — briefly, and only as long as someone keeps paying attention.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Geography 0460 syllabus (2027–2029), topics 3.1–3.4; IMBIE Antarctic ice-mass balance (Nature, 2023); CCAMLR and Antarctic Treaty Secretariat records; INPE PRODES deforestation data; IAATO visitor statistics.</p>
</TopicPage>
